<urn:atm:triage:finding/RBQA-AI28-3>
  a triage:Finding ;
  triage:findingId "RBQA-AI28-3" ;
  triage:title "Background retry scheduling doesn't compose cleanly with router terminal/unconfirmed classification" ;
  triage:description "The bounded peer recovery coordinator schedules backoff-based retries independently, but the router's terminal (permanent failure) vs. unconfirmed (transient, recoverable) classification is not always synchronized with the coordinator's retry schedule. In edge cases, the coordinator might schedule a retry for a peer whose delivery status has been classified as terminal by the router, or vice versa. This is a composition gap between two independent systems that should be coordinated: if a delivery is truly terminal (e.g., peer address resolution failed), the coordinator should not retry indefinitely; conversely, if the coordinator decides a peer is worth retrying, that decision should influence the router's classification to prevent marking deliveries as permanent failures prematurely. This is not an immediate functional bug but a design coherence issue that should be resolved to prevent future divergence. Fix recommended: clarify and document the ownership boundary between the router's classification and the coordinator's retry logic; ensure that terminal deliveries bypass coordinator retry scheduling, and that the coordinator's retry decisions are visible to routing decisions." ;
  triage:category "Architecture" ;
  triage:severity "Minor" ;
  triage:phaseId "phase-AI" ;
  triage:triageMode "initial_pass" ;
  triage:repeatable true ;
  triage:sweepScope "crate" ;
  triage:status "open" ;
  triage:dispatchReady false ;
